        <div id="safety-and-durability" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Safety and Durability: What to Check Before You Fold</h2>
        <p>
          A folding treadmill introduces a few safety considerations that
          fixed-deck machines simply don't have, and they're worth
          understanding before your first fold, not after a close call.
        </p>
        <h3>Auto-Lock Mechanisms</h3>
        <p>
          Most quality folding treadmills include an auto-lock or safety pin
          that has to be deliberately released before the deck can fold down.
          This is not a feature to skip when comparing models. It's the
          single biggest protection against the deck accidentally dropping
          while someone is walking past, reaching underneath, or standing
          nearby. Always confirm you feel or hear the lock engage fully when
          you raise the deck, every single time.
        </p>
        <h3>Inspecting Hinges Over Time</h3>
        <p>
          The fold hinge is a moving mechanical part, and like any moving
          part, it's worth a periodic visual check. Every few weeks, look for
          any new looseness, unusual noise when folding, or visible wear
          around the hinge pins and bolts. Keeping the hinge area free
          of dust and giving it a light dry lubricant every couple of months
          helps keep the fold feeling as smooth as it did on day one.
        </p>
        <h3>Weight Capacity Margins</h3>
        <p>
          Never buy a folding treadmill right at your exact body weight
          limit. We'd recommend leaving at least a <strong>
            20-30 pound buffer
          </strong> below the stated capacity, since that number reflects the
          absolute upper bound under ideal conditions, not a comfortable
          long-term operating range. This matters even more on folding
          treadmills specifically, since lighter frame construction is often
          part of how manufacturers achieve a smaller folded footprint.
        </p>
        <h3>Pinch Points Around Kids and Pets</h3>
        <p>
          A folded treadmill standing upright presents a real tipping and
          pinch-point hazard in households with young kids or curious pets.
          The hinge area can pinch small fingers, and an improperly locked
          unit can fall forward if bumped. If you have kids or pets, store
          the folded treadmill against a wall in a spot with limited traffic,
          and consider models with a floor-level safety latch in addition to
          the primary fold lock.
        </p>
        <p>
          Durability over the long haul also comes down to habits that have
          nothing to do with the fold mechanism itself. Wiping down the deck
          and rails after sweaty sessions, keeping the belt properly
          tensioned and lubricated per the manufacturer's schedule, and
          avoiding placing the folded unit somewhere it will be exposed to
          direct sun or a heat vent all extend the life of the electronics
          and the frame alike. Skipped belt maintenance wears a treadmill out
          faster, and the fold hinge usually isn't the weak link people
          assume it is.
        </p>

        <div class="not-prose bg-red-50 border border-red-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
          <p class="font-black text-red-900 mb-3">Common Mistakes to Avoid</p>
